mb-boundary-vs-judge
--------------------

This directory is a clean-room Python 3 standard-library method sketch. It is
original work for this repository and contains no third-party code, data,
traces, prompts, labels or weights.

Paper (method idea credited)
----------------------------
Sungbin Im.
"When Evaluators of AI Agents Disagree: An Empirical Study of Native
Scoring, LLM Judges, and Commit-Boundary Checks on tau2-bench".
arXiv:2611.14581, v1, submitted 10 Nov 2026. Primary category cs.SE,
cross-listed cs.AI and cs.CR.
https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14581
Paper licence: arXiv non-exclusive distribution licence,
http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/ (the paper is not under
CC BY; only the abstract is quoted or used).

Replication package
-------------------
Zenodo, DOI 10.5281/zenodo.23683806 (artifact_v9.zip).
Licence: code under code/ is MIT ("Copyright (c) 2026 Sungbin Im"); data,
annotations and documents are CC BY 4.0,
https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/.
The package was not run. Only its README and LICENSE were read, during the
research pack, for licence facts. No package code, trace, prompt, label or
output was copied, consulted or used as data for this sketch, and the
tau2-bench benchmark it cites was not used. The package is UNVERIFIED here.

Method only: the idea of scoring the same episodes with several evaluators
(native gold-state scoring, a transcript judge, and a model-free boundary
check of whether a write was expected against whether one occurred), and
the abstract's candidate cause that a gate's evidence digest dropped order
status and customer authentication. The text-only judge stub, its cue rule
and noise, the boundary classes, the toy gate and its fallback, the oracle
and the fixtures are this sketch's own; no judge prompt or model is used.
No text, figures, tables or data from the paper are copied into the code;
the PDF was not read; no model was run. The paper's claims appear only in
paper_evidence.json, quoted and marked UNVERIFIED.
